            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>The Statutory Framework: MMDR Act, 1957 & Constitutional Allocation</h3>\n            <p>In India, the governance, conservation, exploration, and development of all mineral resources (excluding mineral oil, petroleum, and natural gas) are anchored upon a single foundational statutory pillar: the <strong>Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 (MMDR Act, 1957)</strong>. Enacted by Parliament pursuant to Entry 54 of List I (Union List) of the Seventh Schedule to the Constitution of India, the Act establishes a unified federal architecture balancing national strategic priorities with state-level revenue autonomy.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Constitutional Matrix: Seventh Schedule Division</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Entry 54, List I (Union List):</strong> Empowers the Union Parliament to regulate mines and mineral development to the extent to which such regulation is declared by Parliament by law to be expedient in the public interest.</li>\n                <li><strong>Entry 23, List II (State List):</strong> Grants State Legislatures jurisdiction over the regulation of mines and mineral development, <em>strictly subject to the provisions of List I with respect to regulation and development under the control of the Union</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Constitutional Supremacy:</strong> Once Parliament enacted the MMDR Act, 1957, declaring central control in the public interest, the legislative field for major minerals became an occupied field, vesting overarching regulatory and policy authority in the Central Government.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Core Objectives & Institutional Architecture of MMDR Act, 1957</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Uniform Mineral Administration:</strong> Lays down standard tenures, conditions, royalty frameworks, and procedural rules for granting mineral concessions across all states.</li>\n                <li><strong>Prevention of Unscientific Exploitation:</strong> Mandates systematic mine planning, progressive mine closure plans (PMCP), and environmental clearances (under the EPA, 1986) to prevent reckless environmental despoliation.</li>\n                <li><strong>Central Regulatory Organs:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Ministry of Mines:</strong> Apex executive policymaking ministry of the Union Government.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM):</strong> Subordinate technical organization (headquartered in Nagpur) responsible for approving Mining Plans, conducting statutory inspections, enforcing Mineral Conservation and Development Rules (MCDR), and maintaining national mineral statistics.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Geological Survey of India (GSI):</strong> Premier scientific agency (headquartered in Kolkata) mandated for baseline geological mapping and regional mineral resource assessments.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
