            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Classification of Minerals: Major vs Minor & Federal Power Distribution</h3>\n            <p>A distinctive and frequently examined feature of Indian mineral jurisprudence is the statutory bifurcation of all minerals into two legal categories under Section 3(e) of the MMDR Act, 1957: <strong>Major Minerals</strong> and <strong>Minor Minerals</strong>. This classification is not based on geological abundance, but represents a deliberate constitutional distribution of administrative, regulatory, and revenue-raising powers between the Union and the States.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Minor Minerals: Definition & Absolute State Sovereignty</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Statutory Definition (Section 3(e)):</strong> Includes building stones, gravel, ordinary clay, ordinary sand (other than sand used for industrial purposes like glassmaking or foundry), boulder, shingle, chalcedony pebbles, slate, marble, granite, kankar, and limestone used in lime kilns.</li>\n                <li><strong>Power of State Governments (Section 15):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Section 15 of the MMDR Act vests <strong>plenary, exclusive powers in State Governments</strong> to make rules for regulating the grant of quarry leases, mining leases, and concessions for minor minerals.</li>\n                        <li>State Governments fix the <strong>royalty rates, dead rent, concession terms, application fees, and environmental safeguards</strong> for minor minerals without requiring any Central concurrence!</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Revenues:</strong> 100% of all royalties, dead rents, auction premiums, and penalties from minor minerals accrue directly into the Consolidated Fund of the respective State.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Major Minerals: Centralized Statutory Control</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Definition:</strong> Any mineral that is <em>not</em> specified as a minor mineral (e.g., Iron Ore, Bauxite, Manganese, Copper, Lead, Zinc, Gold, Chromite, Limestone, Gypsum, Mica, Coal, Lignite, and Atomic Minerals).</li>\n                <li><strong>Union Powers:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>The Central Government prescribes uniform royalty rates (under the Second Schedule to the MMDR Act), terms of auction, bidding parameters, and environmental conservation rules.</li>\n                        <li>For specified minerals in the <strong>First Schedule</strong> (such as Atomic Minerals and Hydrocarbons), no mining lease or composite license can be granted by a State Government without the <strong>prior approval of the Central Government</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Landmark Notification of 2015: De-notifying 31 Minerals as 'Minor'</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>In February 2015, the Ministry of Mines took a historic decentralization step by notifying <strong>31 minerals as 'Minor Minerals'</strong> (including barite, dolomite, gypsum, mica, silica sand, pyrophyllite, quartz, and soapstone).</li>\n                <li>This transferred complete regulatory and licensing authority over these 31 industrial minerals to State Governments, empowering states to tailor concession rules to local industries.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
