            "title": "District Mineral Foundation (DMF) & PMKKKY: Mining-Affected Communities",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>District Mineral Foundation (DMF) & PMKKKY: Empowering Mining-Affected People</h3>\n            <p>One of the most compassionate and revolutionary socio-economic innovations of the MMDR Amendment Act, 2015 was the statutory creation of the <strong>District Mineral Foundation (DMF)</strong> under <strong>Section 9B</strong> of the Act. For the first time in Indian history, the law mandated that local mining-affected communities—predominantly tribal and marginalized forest-dwellers who historically bore the health, social, and ecological burdens of resource extraction without sharing in its wealth—are granted direct, non-lapsable development funds generated from the minerals extracted from their ancestral lands.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Institutional & Funding Structure of DMF</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Statutory Nature:</strong> A non-profit trust established by the respective State Government in <strong>every district affected by mining-related operations</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Funding Mechanism (Mandatory Contributions by Miners):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Leases Auctioned Post-2015:</strong> Leaseholders contribute an additional amount equivalent to <strong>10% of the statutory royalty</strong> paid to the State Government.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Leases Granted Prior to 2015:</strong> Leaseholders contribute an amount equivalent to <strong>30% of the statutory royalty</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Colossal Financial Corpus:</strong> Since its inception, DMF trusts nationwide have collected over <strong>₹85,000 to ₹90,000 crore (over $10–$11 billion)</strong>, making it one of the largest decentralized local development funds in the developing world! Leading collecting states include <strong>Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Rajasthan, and Madhya Pradesh</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Pradhan Mantri Khanij Kshetra Kalyan Yojana (PMKKKY)</h4>\n            <p>Launched by the Ministry of Mines in September 2015, the <strong>PMKKKY</strong> provides the mandatory national operational guidelines for utilizing DMF funds, dividing expenditure into strict priority quotas:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. High-Priority Sectors (At Least 60% of Total DMF Funds):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Drinking Water Supply:</strong> Piped potable water, RO plants, and tube wells in fluoride/iron-contaminated mining tracts.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Healthcare:</strong> Mobile medical units, multi-specialty trauma centers, screening for silicosis and occupational pulmonary diseases.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Education:</strong> School buildings, nutritional supplements, residential hostels for tribal children.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Environmental Preservation & Pollution Control:</strong> Afforestation of mine dumps, dust suppression mist-cannons, sewage treatment.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Welfare of Women, Children & Aged:</strong> Anganwadi centers, nutrition, care for disabled.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Other Priority Sectors (Up to 40% of Total DMF Funds):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Physical infrastructure, rural all-weather roads and bridges, irrigation dams, watershed management, alternative skill development, and energy connectivity.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
