            "id": "m06",
            "title": "MMDR Amendment Act, 2021: Dismantling the Captive Divide & Transferability",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>The MMDR Amendment Act, 2021: Unlocking Market Efficiencies & Concession Liquidity</h3>\n            <p>Following the structural e-auction reforms of 2015, the Indian mining sector faced operational bottlenecks, including rigid restrictions on captive mines and cumbersome transfer procedures for mineral concessions. To stimulate private investment, eliminate artificial market segmentation, and accelerate mineral production, Parliament enacted the <strong>MMDR Amendment Act, 2021 (effective March 28, 2021)</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Dismantling the Captive vs Non-Captive Dichotomy</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Historical Distortion:</strong> Previously, a 'Captive Mine' (allocated to a specific steel, power, or cement plant) was strictly barred by law from selling its excavated mineral ore in the open commercial market, even if it held vast surplus capacity. Meanwhile, non-captive merchant mines faced impending lease expirations, causing domestic raw material crunches.</li>\n                <li><strong>The 2021 Reform (Section 8B):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Completely <strong>erased the legal distinction between captive and merchant mines</strong>. No future mineral auctions are restricted to captive use.</li>\n                        <li>Existing captive mines are empowered to <strong>sell up to 50% of their annual mineral production in the open commercial market</strong>, after meeting their internal plant requirements, upon payment of an additional prescribed amount to the State Government.</li>\n                        <li>Unlocks millions of tonnes of surplus iron ore, limestone, and bauxite into the national market, preventing price volatility and curbing imports.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Seamless Transferability of Mineral Concessions</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Under the 2021 amendment, all mineral concessions (Mining Leases and Composite Licences) can be <strong>freely transferred to third parties without requiring any prior government approval or transfer charges</strong>.</li>\n                <li>Fosters a dynamic secondary market for mineral assets, allowing distressed or non-performing assets to be acquired by financially robust operators.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Statutory Continuity: Seamless Clearance Transfer</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>When an existing mine is auctioned upon lease expiry, all statutory approvals—including <strong>Environment Clearance (EC) and Forest Clearance (FC)</strong>—are automatically deemed transferred to the new successful bidder for a period of <strong>two years</strong>, preventing devastating production shutdowns during administrative transitions.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "MMDR Amendment Act, 2021 removed the legal distinction between captive and non-captive/merchant mines.",
                "Captive mines can sell up to 50% of annual output in the open market upon paying prescribed additional fees.",
                "Mineral concessions can be freely transferred without prior government permission or transfer charges.",
                "Existing Environment and Forest Clearances (EC/FC) automatically transfer to the new auction winner for 2 years.",
                "Streamlines operational continuity and prevents raw material shortages during lease transitions.",
                "Empowered Central Government to conduct auctions if State Governments fail to complete them within designated timelines."
            ],
