            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>The National Critical Minerals Strategy: 30 Critical Minerals & Supply Chain Vulnerabilities</h3>\n            <p>In June 2023, the Ministry of Mines released a landmark policy blueprint titled <strong>'Critical Minerals for India'</strong>, authored by an expert committee. The report represents India’s strategic response to the global geopolitical realignment, the accelerating global clean energy transition, and weaponized supply chain disruptions. The committee evaluated minerals based on two core scientific metrics: <strong>Economic Importance</strong> and <strong>Supply Vulnerability Risk</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The 30 Identified Critical Minerals</h4>\n            <p>The expert committee delineated a master list of <strong>30 Critical Minerals</strong> indispensable for India’s economic sovereignty, defense manufacturing, telecommunications, and net-zero commitments:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Clean Energy & Electric Mobility:</strong> Lithium ($Li$), Cobalt ($Co$), Nickel ($Ni$), Graphite ($C$), Rare Earth Elements ($REE$), Silicon ($Si$), Vanadium ($V$).</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Semiconductors, Advanced Electronics & Telecom:</strong> Gallium ($Ga$), Germanium ($Ge$), Indium ($In$), Tantalum ($Ta$), Tellurium ($Te$), Bismuth ($Bi$), Selenium ($Se$).</li>\n                <li><strong>3. Aerospace, Defense & Superalloys:</strong> Titanium ($Ti$), Niobium ($Nb$), Beryllium ($Be$), Hafnium ($Hf$), Tungsten ($W$), Rhenium ($Re$), Molybdenum ($Mo$), Zirconium ($Zr$).</li>\n                <li><strong>4. Agricultural Food Security:</strong> Phosphorus ($P$) and Potash ($K$).</li>\n                <li><strong>5. Specialized Industrial Infrastructure:</strong> Copper ($Cu$), Tin ($Sn$), Antimony ($Sb$), Platinum Group Elements ($PGE$), Barite, and High-Purity Limestone (SMS grade).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. India’s Acute Supply Chain Vulnerabilities & 100% Import Traps</h4>\n            <table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; width: 100%;\">\n                <tr style=\"background-color: #f0f0f0;\">\n                    <th>Import Dependence Level</th>\n                    <th>Critical Minerals</th>\n                    <th>Dominant Global Source / Supply Chokepoints</th>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>100% Import Dependent</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Lithium, Cobalt, Nickel, Niobium, Tantalum, Beryllium, Germanium, Strontium</strong></td>\n                    <td>\n                        • Lithium: Australia, Chile, Argentina<br>\n                        • Cobalt: DR Congo (mines &gt;70%), China (refines &gt;75%)<br>\n                        • Nickel: Indonesia, Russia, Philippines<br>\n                        • Niobium: Brazil (&gt;85% of world supply)\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>High Import Dependence (80%–95%)</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Copper, Rare Earth Elements, Potash, Gallium, Tungsten, Antimony</strong></td>\n                    <td>\n                        • Copper: Chile, Peru<br>\n                        • Potash: Belarus, Russia, Canada, Jordan<br>\n                        • Gallium & Antimony: China (&gt;60%–80% world refining)\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>3. The Geopolitical Imperative: De-risking from Monopolies</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>The strategy addresses the extreme geographical concentration of processing infrastructure: <strong>China controls over 70% of global lithium refining, 75% of cobalt refining, and over 90% of permanent magnet rare earth processing</strong>.</li>\n                <li>India joined the multilateral <strong>Minerals Security Partnership (MSP)</strong> in June 2023—a US-led coalition of 14 nations committed to building resilient, transparent critical mineral supply chains outside authoritarian monopolies.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
