            "title": "Deep Ocean Mission (DOM) & Polymetallic Nodules: Central Indian Ocean Basin",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Deep Ocean Mission (DOM) & Polymetallic Nodules: India’s Oceanic Mineral Frontier</h3>\n            <p>Beneath the abyssal plains of the world’s oceans, thousands of meters below the reach of sunlight, lies the next frontier of global resource competition: <strong>Deep-Sea Mining</strong>. To harness the immense scientific, economic, and mineral potential of the ocean floor, the Government of India launched the multi-disciplinary <strong>Deep Ocean Mission (DOM)</strong> under the Ministry of Earth Sciences (MoES), approved by the Union Cabinet in August 2021 with a financial outlay of over <strong>₹4,077 crore</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. India’s Pioneer Investor Status & International Seabed Authority (ISA) Allocation</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Pioneer Investor (1987):</strong> Under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), India was the <strong>first developing country in the world to be accorded the status of a 'Pioneer Investor'</strong> in deep seabed exploration by the International Seabed Authority (ISA).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Central Indian Ocean Basin (CIOB) Concession:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>India holds exclusive exploration rights granted by the ISA over a designated area of <strong>75,000 square kilometers</strong> in the <strong>Central Indian Ocean Basin (CIOB)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Located at water depths ranging between <strong>4,000 to 6,000 meters</strong>, situated approximately 2,000 kilometers south of the Indian peninsula.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Polymetallic Nodules (PMN): The Subsea Treasury</h4>\n            <p><strong>Polymetallic Nodules</strong> (also called Manganese Nodules) are dark-brown, potato-sized concretions formed over millions of years by the slow concentric precipitation of dissolved hydrothermal and hydrogenous metal ions around microscopic cores (shark teeth, volcanic shards) on the abyssal sediment plain:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Estimated Reserves in India's Allocated CIOB Site:</strong> Estimated at <strong>380 million tonnes of polymetallic nodules</strong>!</li>\n                <li><strong>Critical Metal Endowment:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Manganese ($Mn$):</strong> ~24% to 25% of nodule mass (approx. <strong>92 million tonnes</strong>).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Nickel ($Ni$):</strong> ~1.2% to 1.3% (approx. <strong>4.7 million tonnes</strong>) — strategic for EV battery cathodes!</li>\n                        <li><strong>Copper ($Cu$):</strong> ~1.0% to 1.1% (approx. <strong>4.3 million tonnes</strong>).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Cobalt ($Co$):</strong> ~0.24% to 0.25% (approx. <strong>1.0 million tonnes</strong>) — one of the rarest, most expensive energy metals on Earth.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Strategic Impact:</strong> Extraction from just 10% of India's CIOB site could meet the nation's entire energy and battery demand for the next 50 years, liberating India from foreign supply chokepoints.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
