            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Polymetallic Massive Sulphides (PMS): Mineral Wealth of Underwater Volcanoes</h3>\n            <p>In addition to polymetallic nodules on abyssal plains, the ocean floor harbors another extraordinarily concentrated class of mineral deposits: <strong>Polymetallic Massive Sulphides (PMS)</strong>. These deposits form along active tectonic spreading centers at <strong>Mid-Ocean Ridges (MOR)</strong>, where volcanic magma chambers heat circulating seawater into mineral-rich hydrothermal plumes.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. India's Second ISA Exploration Contract: 10,000 Square Kilometers</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>In 2016, India signed its <strong>second 15-year exploration contract with the International Seabed Authority (ISA)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Concession:</strong> Covers <strong>10,000 square kilometers</strong> distributed in 100 individual blocks (10 km x 10 km each) along the:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Central Indian Ridge (CIR)</strong> and the</li>\n                        <li><strong>Southwest Indian Ridge (SWIR)</strong> in the southern Indian Ocean.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Formation Mechanics: The 'Black Smokers'</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Hydrothermal Circulation:</strong> Cold seawater seeps into deep fractures near oceanic spreading ridges, sinks miles down, and is heated to <strong>350°C to 400°C</strong> by underlying magma chambers.</li>\n                <li><strong>Leaching & Eruption:</strong> The superheated, acidic, oxygen-depleted fluid leaches copper, zinc, gold, and iron from oceanic basalt rocks. Upon erupting through hydrothermal vents ('chimneys' or <strong>Black Smokers</strong>) into cold (2°C) deep seawater, dissolved metal sulfides instantly precipitate out, accumulating into massive mound-like ore bodies on the seafloor.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Grade & Strategic Commodity Endowment of PMS</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Unlike low-grade land mines, seafloor massive sulphides are <strong>exceptionally high-grade ore deposits</strong>:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Copper ($Cu$):</strong> Often exceeds <strong>5% to 10% grade</strong> (compared to 0.5%–1.0% in open-pit land mines!).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Zinc ($Zn$):</strong> 10% to 20% concentration.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Precious Metals:</strong> Substantial grades of <strong>Gold ($Au$)</strong> (often 2–10 grams per tonne) and <strong>Silver ($Ag$)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Lead ($Pb$)</strong> and associated trace elements like Gallium, Germanium, and Indium.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Unique Chemosynthetic Ecosystems</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Hydrothermal vents support extraordinary, alien-like ecosystems operating entirely independent of sunlight, powered by <strong>chemosynthetic sulfur-oxidizing bacteria</strong> sustaining giant tube worms, vent crabs, and blind shrimp. India’s exploration protocols include stringent environmental baseline monitoring to protect these vulnerable marine ecosystems.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
