            "id": "m17",
            "title": "Sustainable Mining, Environmental Jurisprudence & Supreme Court Doctrines",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Sustainable Mining, Environmental Jurisprudence & Supreme Court Doctrines</h3>\n            <p>Mining in India operates at the fragile intersection of economic development, tribal rights, forest conservation, and environmental sustainability. Because minerals are finite, non-renewable natural endowments that often underlie ancient ecological hotspots and ancestral tribal habitats (Fifth Schedule areas), the Supreme Court of India and Parliament have evolved a sophisticated body of <strong>Environmental and Natural Resource Jurisprudence</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Foundational Constitutional Doctrines</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. The Public Trust Doctrine (<em>M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath, 1997</em>):</strong> The Supreme Court affirmed that natural resources—including forests, rivers, air, and subterranean minerals—are held by the State in public trust for the enjoyment of the general public and future generations. The State cannot alienate or exploit them in a manner that causes wanton ecological degradation.</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Intergenerational Equity:</strong> Enshrined in the <em>Goa Foundation Case (2014)</em>, this doctrine mandates that the present generation must act as responsible trustees of mineral wealth, extracting resources at sustainable thresholds so as not to disinherit future unborn generations.</li>\n                <li><strong>3. Precautionary Principle & Polluter Pays Principle:</strong> The mining operator bears absolute strict liability for all environmental degradation, acid mine drainage, deforestation, and water pollution caused by mining operations.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Landmark Judgment: Common Cause v. Union of India (2017) (Odisha Mining Scam)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>In August 2017, the Supreme Court delivered a historic verdict on rampant illegal mining in Keonjhar and Sundargarh districts of Odisha, investigated by the Justice M.B. Shah Commission.</li>\n                <li><strong>100% Compensation Recovery (Section 21(5)):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>The Court held that any mineral excavated without statutory <strong>Environment Clearance (EC)</strong> under the EPA 1986, or in excess of approved mining plan limits, constitutes <strong>illegal mining</strong> under Section 21(5) of the MMDR Act.</li>\n                        <li>The Court ordered the recovery of <strong>100% of the market value of the illegally extracted mineral</strong> from errant mining companies! Over <strong>₹17,000 crore</strong> was recovered by the Odisha State Government, establishing an unprecedented global precedent for mining accountability.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Sustainable Development Framework (SDF) & Star Rating of Mines</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>The Ministry of Mines and IBM institutionalized a comprehensive <strong>Sustainable Development Framework (SDF)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Star Rating of Mines (1 to 5 Stars):</strong> A digital, self-evaluation-cum-inspection system evaluating mines on environmental reclamation, green energy adoption, CSR expenditure, local employment, dust mitigation, and zero-effluent discharge. Five-star rated mines are granted fast-track regulatory clearances.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Indian mining operates under the Public Trust Doctrine and Intergenerational Equity.",
                "State is a trustee of natural mineral wealth; exploitation must preserve rights of future generations.",
                "In Common Cause v. Union of India (2017), the Supreme Court mandated 100% value recovery for illegal mining under Section 21(5).",
                "Over ₹17,000 crore was recovered from errant mining companies in Odisha under the landmark 2017 verdict.",
                "Mining requires mandatory Environmental Clearance (EC, EIA 2006) and Forest Clearance (FC, FCA 1980).",
                "Star Rating of Mines (1 to 5 Stars) rewards mines adopting scientific conservation, dust control, and afforestation."
            ],
