            "title": "Comprehensive Review & Comparative Mineral Economics of India",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Comprehensive Review: Comparative Mineral Economics of India</h3>\n            <p>India’s geological endowment is a magnificent tapestry of Precambrian crystalline shields, Proterozoic sedimentary basins, Gondwana rift troughs, Deccan volcanic flood basalts, and Tertiary coastal placers. As the world’s fifth largest economy, commanding an ambitious vision of becoming a $10 trillion industrial superpower with 500 GW of non-fossil energy by 2030, a clear quantitative mastery of India's <strong>Comparative Mineral Economics</strong> is indispensable for Civil Services aspirants.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. India's Global Mineral Leadership Scorecard</h4>\n            <table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; width: 100%;\">\n                <tr style=\"background-color: #f0f0f0;\">\n                    <th>Global Rank</th>\n                    <th>Mineral Commodity / Industrial Indicator</th>\n                    <th>Key Indian States / Industrial Corridors</th>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>#1 in the World</strong></td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>Sheet Mica</strong> production & export<br>\n                        • <strong>Barite</strong> reserves and production<br>\n                        • <strong>Thorium</strong> reserves (hosted in coastal Monazite beach placers)\n                    </td>\n                    <td>\n                        • Sheet Mica: Koderma (Jharkhand), Nellore (AP)<br>\n                        • Barite: Mangampet (Cuddapah, AP)<br>\n                        • Monazite: Kerala, Odisha, TN, AP coasts\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>#2 in the World</strong></td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>Crude Steel</strong> production (&gt;140 MT/year)<br>\n                        • <strong>Cement</strong> production (&gt;380 MT/year)<br>\n                        • <strong>Coal</strong> production and consumption (&gt;1 Billion Tonnes)\n                    </td>\n                    <td>\n                        • Steel: Odisha, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh<br>\n                        • Cement: Rajasthan, MP, AP, Karnataka<br>\n                        • Coal: Jharkhand, Odisha, Chhattisgarh\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>#4 in the World</strong></td>\n                    <td>• <strong>Iron Ore</strong> production (~275 MT/year)</td>\n                    <td>• Odisha (&gt;54%), Chhattisgarh, Karnataka, Jharkhand</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>#5 in the World</strong></td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>Bauxite</strong> geological reserves<br>\n                        • <strong>Rare Earth Elements (REE)</strong> reserves (~6.9 MT)\n                    </td>\n                    <td>\n                        • Bauxite: Odisha (&gt;53%), AP, Gujarat<br>\n                        • REE: Odisha Sands, Kerala, Tamil Nadu placers\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>2. India’s Acute Structural Deficits: 100% Import Dependencies</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Energy & High-Tech Transition Metals:</strong> 100% import-dependent on <strong>Lithium, Cobalt, Nickel, Niobium, Tantalum, Beryllium, Germanium, and Vanadium</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Metallurgical & Agricultural Inputs:</strong> <strong>Coking Coal</strong> (imports >85% of prime low-ash coking coal from Australia) and <strong>Potash ($K_2O$)</strong> (100% imported from Canada, Russia, Jordan, Belarus).</li>\n                <li><strong>Precious Metals:</strong> Heavy net importer of <strong>Gold ($Au$)</strong> and <strong>Platinum Group Elements ($PGE$)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Multi-Pronged Future Roadmap for Indian Mineral Security</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Deep Subsurface Exploration:</strong> NMET funding, private exploration agencies, and the new <strong>Exploration Licence (EL)</strong> to unlock concealed deep-seated ore bodies.</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Decisive Legal Liberalization:</strong> MMDR Amendments (2015, 2021, 2023) dismantling captive restrictions, de-listing atomic minerals, and instituting competitive e-auctions.</li>\n                <li><strong>3. International Asset Acquisitions:</strong> <strong>KABIL</strong> securing overseas lithium brine blocks in Argentina's Catamarca province and critical mineral pacts in Australia.</li>\n                <li><strong>4. Maritime Frontiers:</strong> Offshore Areas Mineral Act, 2023, and the <strong>Deep Ocean Mission</strong> (MATSYA 6000, 75,000 sq km PMN site in the CIOB, 10,000 sq km PMS on Indian ridges).</li>\n                <li><strong>5. Circular Economy & Urban Mining:</strong> Battery Waste Management Rules, 2022, mandating extended producer responsibility (EPR) and minimum recycled critical metal content in new EV batteries by 2030.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
